{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>Two good kids of the goats.</b> She did not command him about two kids of the goats by way of chance, but with a known intention. And thus they said in the Midrash: ״שני גדיי עזים טובים״ — and was Isaac's meal then two kids? R. Berakhiah said: ״שני גדיי עזים טובים״ — good for you, good for your children. Good for you, for through them you will receive the blessings; good for your children, for through them atonement will be made on Yom HaKippurim. He said: good for you — for through these Jacob merited and prevailed over Esau, who was a שעיר (hairy one), as it is said: הן עשו אחי איש שעיר. Good for your children — these are the goats of Yom HaKippurim, through which Israel are atoned for their sin and delivered from the accusing angel of Esau, that he may not prosecute. And thus the Rabbis of blessed memory said (Leviticus 16): ״ונשא השעיר״ — this is Esau; ״את כל עונותם״ — the iniquities of the blameless. And the delicacies that Rebekah commanded Jacob to prepare were not from the beasts that dwell in the field, but from things that dwell in settled habitation. And this is what she said to him: ״Go now to the flock״ — because Jacob was a תם (wholehearted) man, dwelling in tents, and detesting the sword, for such is not the portion of Jacob; and his seed, the receivers of the Torah, were not commanded to bring an offering from the wild beast, but only from the domestic animal, as it is said (Leviticus 1): אדם כי יקריב מכם קרבן לה׳ מן הבהמה וגו׳."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:15:34.822779243Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}